__notes__.note.singularity.setting-paradox-restate:
Hawking's argument: general relativity drives information into the singularity and destroys it. Quantum mechanics: information cannot be destroyed. The chain's finding: both statements are true, at different levels of description, and the contradiction is a category error.

\begin{center}
\emph{Information is lost in $\Cl(1,3)$. Information is preserved in $\Cl(2,4)$. These are the same physics read at two levels, and the discrepancy between the levels is not destruction: it is the fixed projection of Proposition \claimref{singularity.descent-what-it-erases}, extended to the boundary mode space by Definition \claimref{obj.singularity.multi-mode-descent}, and its size is an equation: the relative entropy of Theorem \claimref{singularity.deficit-theorem-cost-descent-exactly}, carried entirely by the correlations of the quanta (Corollary \claimref{singularity.deficit-carried-quanta-correlations}).}
\end{center}

General relativity is right that at the metric level the information is not conserved. Quantum mechanics is right that at the full level it is. The deficit between the two levels is not unaccounted for: traced through Theorem \claimref{singularity.modular-descent}, it is precisely the Hawking flux, thermal at $\TH$, the shadow of the entanglement with the adjoined partner; and its magnitude is not estimated but computed, $\rel{\rho}{\EN\rho} = C(\rho) - C(\EN\rho)$, the correlations the descent cannot carry (Corollary \claimref{singularity.deficit-carried-quanta-correlations}; the section “The descent, applied: the two levels in numbers” evaluates it for the model in exact arithmetic). The paradox does not need resolving; it needs the two levels computed separately. What remains a genuine question is the modern sharp form: whether the exterior radiation is unitary \emph{by itself} and follows the Page curve, i.e.\ whether the projection's deficit is returned to the radiation before the end. That question this section takes as far as the two levels allow.

__notes__.note.state-selection.setting-notation-st:
Throughout, $\Aa$ denotes the completed ladder algebra of the singular locus: at a single point of the blown-up singular boundary $E \cong \R \times S^{2}$ the algebra generated by the Rule 1 pair $(v, v^{*})$ with
\begin{equation}
v^{2} = (v^{*})^{2} = 0, \qquad v v^{*} + v^{*} v = 1 ,
\end{equation}
so that $\Aa \cong \Mat_2(\C)$ as a factor, and over all of $E$ the CAR algebra generated by one such pair per point \cite[Thm.~8.7, Thm.~8.12]{ButlerT4}. We write $N = v^{*}v$ for the number operator, $P = N$ for the occupation projector, $\sigma = v^{*}v - vv^{*} = 2N - 1$ for the modular bivector of \cite[Thm.~6.3]{ButlerT5}, and $K = \sigma/2 = N - \tfrac12$ for the boost generator. The descent is
\begin{equation}
E(x) \;=\; PxP + (1-P)\,x\,(1-P)
\end{equation}
of \cite[Prop.~5.5]{ButlerT5}, and $\EN$ its multi-mode composition \cite[Def.~9.1]{ButlerT5}. Mode labels $k$ run over the ledger; $\omega_k > 0$ is the mode's boost weight. All statements about $\Aa$ at a single point are finite dimensional and are proved outright; the statements that need the infinite system are flagged where they occur. Units and curvature conventions are T5's.

