hawking.modular-structure-completed-pair.formal:
Let $(v, v^*)$ be a completed pair and $\sigma := v^*v - vv^*$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\sigma^2 = 1$; in the hyperbolic atom $\Cl(1,1)$ with $v = \tfrac12(e_0 + e_1)$, $v^* = \tfrac12(e_0 - e_1)$, one has $\sigma = e_0 e_1$: the unit boost bivector of the plane (relabelling the pair reverses the orientation);
\item the rotor flow $R_\lambda := \exp(\lambda\sigma/2)$ acts by $R_\lambda\, v\, R_\lambda^{-1} = e^{-\lambda}v$ and $R_\lambda\, v^*\, R_\lambda^{-1} = e^{+\lambda}v^*$: the pair diagonalises the boost with opposite weights;
\item the spectral projectors $\tfrac12(1 \pm \sigma)$ are the occupation projectors $v^*v$ and $vv^*$;
\item in the Fock representation the boost generator is the shifted number operator, $\tfrac12\sigma = N - \tfrac12$;
\item the bivector space of $\Cl(1,1)$ is one-dimensional, so the boost flow is the unique one-parameter rotor flow of the atom \emph{up to reparametrisation of the flow parameter and orientation}: the algebra fixes the orbits of any rotor-implemented modular flow, but not its speed, which is exactly the normalisation Remark \claimref{note.hawking.what-now-proved-open-problem} (the section “The state input: the I-state”) leaves open.
\end{enumerate}

singularity.chains-state-class-stability.formal:
Let $\mathcal{C}$ be the set of even states of the boundary modes every single-mode marginal of which is the Gibbs state of $K_k$ at $\beta = 2\pi/\kappa$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item under (P) and (R) the state of the boundary modes lies in $\mathcal{C}$, and every member of $\mathcal{C}$ satisfies (P) and (R);
\item $\mathcal{C}$ is invariant under every internal dynamics that (L$_E$) permits: a state placed in $\mathcal{C}$ at injection stays in $\mathcal{C}$ thereafter, whatever the boundary modes write;
\item $\mathcal{C}$ contains the product state $e^{-\beta K_{\mathrm{geo}}}/Z$, which is its only member satisfying (G), and it contains states of arbitrarily large total correlation $C(\varrho)$ and descent deficit; (R) constrains none of the correlations.
\end{enumerate}
So the premises fix the marginals once and for all, and leave the joint state exactly as free as Steps 5 and 6b need it; the rate model then exercises that freedom across the window/radiation cut, its window marginal being itself the product member at every stage (Theorem \claimref{singularity.eviction-window-thermal-product-radiation-entropy}(1)).

singularity.modular-structure-completed-.formal:
Let $(v, v^*)$ satisfy $v^2 = (v^*)^2 = 0, \qquad v v^* + v^* v = 1$ and define $\sigma := v^* v - v v^*$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\sigma^2 = 1$; and in the hyperbolic atom of Lemma \claimref{singularity.pseudo-inverse-identities-hyperbolic-atom}, $\sigma = e_0 e_1$ exactly, the unit boost bivector of the plane;
\item the rotor flow $R_\lambda = \exp(\lambda\sigma/2)$ acts by $v \mapsto e^{-\lambda} v$ and $v^* \mapsto e^{+\lambda} v^*$: the pair diagonalises the boost with opposite weights, the structure of outgoing and ingoing horizon modes;
\item the spectral projectors $\tfrac12(1 \pm \sigma)$ of the boost are identically the occupation projectors $v^* v$ and $v v^*$: the descent projectors of Proposition \claimref{singularity.descent-what-it-erases};
\item in the fermionic representation $\sigma = 2N - 1$: the generator of the geometric boost is, up to normalisation, the number operator, $K := \tfrac{\sigma}{2} = N - \tfrac12$;
\item $\mathrm{Spin}^+(1,1)$ is one-dimensional, so the boost flow is the \emph{only} one-parameter flow implemented by real rotors of the atom, up to reparametrisation of the flow parameter and orientation. The scope matters and is stated: the mode algebra $\Mat_2(\C)$ admits a three-parameter family of one-parameter unitary groups, and the uniqueness claimed is within the class the geometry supplies, real rotor flows of $\Cl(1,1)$; Proposition \claimref{singularity.euclidean-rotor-where-it-lies} later leaves that class deliberately, its Euclidean rotor being complex.
\end{enumerate}
One object, three readings: boost generator, number operator, generator of the descent projectors.

singularity.pinching-against-partial-tra.formal:
\leavevmode
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] (One mode: they agree.) For any global state whose Schmidt basis across the pair/partner cut is the number basis (as boost invariance forces in Lemma \claimref{singularity.schmidt-forcing-proved-outright}, and as the support constraint forces in the model, Lemma \claimref{singularity.parity-protection}), the mode marginal $\tr_{\mathrm{partner}}\,\rho$ is diagonal in the number basis and hence a fixed point of $E$: partial trace and pinching coincide, mode by mode.
\item[(ii)] (Many modes: they differ, by exactly the inter-mode coherence.) On $N \geq 2$ modes, $\tr_L$ and $\EN$ are different maps: $\tr_L$ retains the radiation's off-diagonal joint-number matrix elements, $\EN$ annihilates them, and the two agree on every single-mode marginal. The two maps answer different questions: $\tr_L$ asks what the radiation alone contains; $\EN$ asks what the metric level can carry. Conflating them is harmless for single-mode spectra and fatal for the computation of correlations, which is why the conflation is scoped once, in this step: Step 4d's identification uses clause (i), and Step 6b's computation uses clause (ii).
\end{enumerate}

singularity.two-kms-functionals-sign.formal:
On the mode factor of the completed pair, whose scalars are $\mathrm{span}\{1, J\}$ with $J = e_{2}e_{3}$ the complex unit of the atom (\claimref{synthesis.transverse-bivector-is-the-complex-unit-of-the-radial-atom}), with flow $\alpha_t = \operatorname{Ad}(e^{J t\omega K})$, let $\Gamma := 1 - 2N = -\sigma$ be the mode's fermion parity, and for homogeneous $b$ write $\epsilon_b = \pm 1$ for even and odd $b$. Fix $\beta > 0$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item The functionals $\phi = \tr(\varrho\,\cdot\,)$ satisfying the \emph{ungraded} KMS identity $\phi(ab) = \phi\bigl(b\,\alpha_{J\beta}(a)\bigr)$ for all $a, b$, with $\alpha_{J\beta}(a) = e^{-\beta\omega K}a\,e^{\beta\omega K}$, are the multiples of $\tr(e^{-\beta\omega K}\,\cdot\,)$; normalised, this is the unique KMS state, with partition function $Z = 1 + e^{-\beta\omega}$ and occupancy $(e^{\beta\omega} + 1)^{-1}$.
\item The functionals satisfying the \emph{graded} identity $\phi(ab) = \epsilon_b\,\phi\bigl(b\,\alpha_{J\beta}(a)\bigr)$ for all $a$ and homogeneous $b$ are the multiples of $\tr(\Gamma e^{-\beta\omega K}\,\cdot\,)$; normalised, this is the parity-twisted functional with $Z_\Gamma = 1 - e^{-\beta\omega}$ and ``occupancy'' $-e^{-\beta\omega}/(1 - e^{-\beta\omega}) < 0$: it is not a state.
\item The $+1$ of the Fermi-Dirac denominator is $Z = 1 + e^{-\beta\omega}$: the two terms are the two states of the module, one per power of $v^{*}$ before $(v^{*})^{2} = 0$, and the sign between them is $+$ for the KMS state and $-$ for the twisted functional. The gluing condition of (R) (Premise \claimref{singularity.r-regularity-fixed-locus}), which reverses the order of $a$ and $b$ without a sign, is the ungraded identity, and selects the state.
\end{enumerate}

state-selection.partner-purification-are.formal:
Let $\omega$ be a faithful state on the mode factor $\Aa \cong \Mat_2(\C)$ (scalars $\mathrm{span}\{1, e_{2}e_{3}\}$, the complex unit of the radial atom, \claimref{synthesis.transverse-bivector-is-the-complex-unit-of-the-radial-atom}; below, $J$ is the modular conjugation of Lemma \claimref{singularity.modular-theory-matrix-factor-proved-outright}, not that unit), with density matrix $\rho = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_0^{2}, \lambda_1^{2})$, $\lambda_i > 0$. Then in the GNS representation of $\omega$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the representation space is $\C^{2}\otimes\C^{2}$, the cyclic vector is $\Omega = \sum_i \lambda_i |ii\rangle$, and $\Omega$ is cyclic and separating for $\pi(\Aa) = \Aa \otimes 1$;
\item the commutant $\pi(\Aa)' = 1 \otimes \Aa$ is the partner copy, and is generated from $\pi(\Aa)$ by the modular conjugation, $\pi(\Aa)' = J\pi(\Aa)J$;
\item any two purifications of $\omega$ are related by a unitary acting on the purifying factor alone, so $\Omega$ is unique up to a unitary in the commutant;
\item when $\omega$ is the Gibbs state at $\beta$, $\Omega$ is the thermofield double, $\lambda_0 = \cos\theta$, $\lambda_1 = \sin\theta$ with $\tan\theta = e^{-\omega_k/2T}$, which is exactly the state produced by \cite[Lem.~7.2]{ButlerT5}.
\end{enumerate}
Consequently the global purity of hypothesis (i), the existence of the partner, and the thermofield form of the global state are consequences of the one-sided state, not independent choices; and the ``two ends of the pair'' of Step 4d are the algebra and its commutant in the state's own GNS representation.

synthesis.state-selection-r-and-le-again.formal:
Let a window of $n$ boundary modes, completed pairs $(a_{k}, a_{k}^{*})$ obeying the canonical anticommutation relations (Proposition \claimref{scrambling.boundary-mode-space-matrix}), carry the Killing flow $\alpha_{t} = \operatorname{Ad}(e^{Jt\sum_{k}\omega_{k}K_{k}})$, $K_{k} = N_{k} - \tfrac12$ (Proposition \claimref{hawking.modular-structure-completed-pair}), its numbers read in $\mathrm{span}\{1, J\}$ with $J = e_{2}e_{3}$ the complex unit of the completed pair's radial atom (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.transverse-bivector-is-the-complex-unit-of-the-radial-atom}), which commutes with the radial plane and with every vector orthogonal to spacetime, hence with every mode (the continuation $t + Jp$ is in that field), with period $p = 2\pi/\kappa$ and $z_{k} = e^{\omega_{k}p}$. For mode operators $a, b$ write $F_{a,b}(t) = \omega(a\,\alpha_{t}(b))$, a finite sum of exponentials in $t$. Premise (R) of the state-selection note (its Premise 5.1, now retired from the store; its statement is recorded with \claimref{singularity.r-regularity-fixed-locus}) asks that the state's two-point functions, continued in the rapidity of the boost flow, be single valued and regular at its fixed locus; T05's Premise (R) (\claimref{singularity.r-regularity-fixed-locus}) asks that each single-mode correlator continue to the Euclidean section with its two orderings glued across the cut, $F_{a,b}(t + Jp) = \omega(\alpha_{t}(b)\,a)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] (one mode, three readings) on a mode of nonzero weight: regularity alone is vacuous (every $F_{a,b}$ is entire); single-valuedness read as strict periodicity, $F_{a,b}(t + Jp) = F_{a,b}(t)$ for both orderings of the ladder, holds for no state (it forces the occupancy to be both $1$ and $0$); single-valuedness read with the orderings glued, as T05 reads it, holds exactly when the occupancy is $n = 1/(z + 1)$, which is T05's (R) on that mode; the ladder correlators do not see $\omega(a)$, and with $\omega(a) = 0$, as (P) (\claimref{singularity.p-univalence}) gives, the marginal is the Gibbs state at $\beta = p$ (Theorem \claimref{singularity.kms-uniqueness-factor}). At zero weight strict periodicity is vacuous and the glued reading forces $n = \tfrac12$ at every period;
\item[(ii)] (the glued reading on the whole window) write (R$_{\mathrm{reg}}$) for the glued reading imposed on every two-point function of mode operators. On mixed correlators, $i \ne j$, it reads $z^{\mp 1}\omega(a_{i}b_{j}) = -\omega(a_{i}b_{j})$ for $b_{j} \in \{a_{j}^{*}, a_{j}\}$, so (R$_{\mathrm{reg}}$) holds if and only if T05's (R) holds on every mode and every inter-mode two-point function vanishes;
\item[(iii)] (strictly between) T05's (R) with the two-point clause of (Q) (\claimref{singularity.q-joint-quasi-freeness-s-wave}) implies (R$_{\mathrm{reg}}$), and (R$_{\mathrm{reg}}$) implies T05's (R); neither converse holds. On two modes with Gibbs marginals: adding a coherence $c \ne 0$ between $\ket{10}$ and $\ket{01}$ gives an even state satisfying T05's (R) with $\omega(a_{1}a_{2}^{*}) = -c$, so (R$_{\mathrm{reg}}$) fails; adding instead a density correlation $d \ne 0$ gives an even state satisfying (R$_{\mathrm{reg}}$) with $\omega(N_{1}N_{2}) - n_{1}n_{2} = d$, so it is not quasi-free and (Q) fails;
\item[(iv)] (the note's rate theorem) on each mode, the Gibbs form at $\beta = 2\pi/\kappa$ with occupancy in $(0,1)$, hence faithful marginals, follows from (R$_{\mathrm{reg}}$) and (P) by (i), without the geometric modular condition, as T05's Theorem \claimref{singularity.rate-discharge-hypothesis-iii} obtains it from T05's (R); the single-mode conclusions of the note's Theorem \claimref{state-selection.rate-discharge-hypothesis-iii} therefore need only T05's (R);
\item[(v)] (the two (L$_E$)) T05's Premise \claimref{singularity.l-boundary-mode-locality} and the note's Premise 7.3 (now retired from the store as a restatement of T05's) generate the dynamics by the boost bivectors $\sigma_{k}$ and their products, the abelian algebra $\mathcal{A}_{\sigma}$ of dimension $2^{n}$, which commutes with every $N_{k}$, contains the cross-point products $\sigma_{j}\sigma_{k}$, and excludes the hopping $a_{j}^{*}a_{k}$ and the pairing $a_{j}^{*}a_{k}^{*}$ ($j \ne k$). The note's clause that no generator couples the ladder at one point to the ladder at another, read as excluding every generator that involves two points, would exclude $\sigma_{j}\sigma_{k}$, which its own first clause admits and its Proposition 7.4, merged into \claimref{singularity.number-diagonal-dynamics-models-scrambling-derived}, uses (the controlled-$Z$); read as excluding transfer of occupation, it is T05's clause. The two premises name one generator class.
\end{enumerate}
So the note's (L$_E$) restates T05's, and the note's (R), in its one satisfiable contentful reading on the boundary-mode algebra, is T05's (R) plus vanishing inter-mode two-point functions.
